# Calibrating a camera against Lightroom

> **Last updated:** 2026-10-25

The app renders most raws close to Lightroom, but not the same: its camera colour is estimated (from the camera's
own JPEG, spectral data or DNG matrices), not matched. `{{cli_binary}} calibrate-lightroom` closes that gap one camera model at
a time. You run it on **your own raws** and **Lightroom's exports of the same photos**. It fits the camera's colour
model and one look per Lightroom profile you exported, and writes **one small file of numbers** that you send to the
project. Once it is added, every user of that camera gets the matched colour, and catalogues imported from Lightroom
pick the matching look for each photo's profile.

`{{cli_binary}}` is the command-line tool shipped with every release (next to the app).

## Running the command-line tool

The desktop app doesn't take commands: the fit runs in **`{{cli_binary}}`**, the command-line tool every release ships
next to it. The examples use release 26.43.7; use the version you downloaded. Each one ends with `--help`, which lists
every subcommand: replace it with `calibrate-lightroom …` from the steps below. Folder names with spaces need quotes
(`"My Photos"`).

### Windows

Open **PowerShell** (Start menu ▸ type *PowerShell*).

- **Installer (`{{binary}}-26.43.7-windows-x64.msi`)**: the tool is installed next to the app, by default in
  `C:\Program Files\{{app}}`:

  ```powershell
  & "C:\Program Files\{{app}}\{{cli_binary}}.exe" --help
  ```

  (If you chose another folder at install, use that one.)
- **Portable (`{{binary}}-26.43.7-windows-x64-portable.zip`)**: unzip it (right-click ▸ *Extract All…*), then in
  PowerShell go to that folder and run the tool from it:

  ```powershell
  cd "$HOME\Downloads\{{binary}}-26.43.7-windows-x64-portable"
  .\{{cli_binary}}.exe --help
  ```

On ARM PCs the files say `arm64` instead of `x64`.

### macOS

The app's disk image holds only the app: download **`{{cli_binary}}-26.43.7-macos-universal.zip`** from the same
release too (signed and notarized, for Apple silicon and Intel). Double-click it in Finder to unzip, then open
**Terminal** (Applications ▸ Utilities) and run it from that folder:

```sh
cd ~/Downloads
./{{cli_binary}} --help
```

To run it from anywhere, move it onto your PATH once: `sudo mv ~/Downloads/{{cli_binary}} /usr/local/bin/`.

### Linux

Open a terminal.

- **Debian/Ubuntu (`.deb`) or Fedora/openSUSE (`.rpm`)**: the package installs the tool on your PATH:

  ```sh
  {{cli_binary}} --help
  ```

- **Tarball (`{{binary}}-26.43.7-linux-x86_64.tar.gz`)**: unpack it and run the tool from its `bin` folder:

  ```sh
  tar xf {{binary}}-26.43.7-linux-x86_64.tar.gz
  ./{{binary}}-26.43.7-linux-x86_64/bin/{{cli_binary}} --help
  ```

- **AppImage**: starting the AppImage starts the app; the tool is inside it. Unpack it once and run the tool from the
  unpacked folder:

  ```sh
  chmod +x {{binary}}-26.43.7-linux-x86_64.AppImage
  ./{{binary}}-26.43.7-linux-x86_64.AppImage --appimage-extract
  ./squashfs-root/usr/bin/{{cli_binary}} --help
  ```

- **Flatpak**: run the tool inside the app's sandbox, giving it your home folder (where the raws and exports are):

  ```sh
  flatpak run --filesystem=home --command={{cli_binary}} {{app_id}} --help
  ```

Every example below writes `{{cli_binary}}`: put the path or prefix you used here in front of it.

## What you need

- 20 or more raws from **one camera model**, as varied as you can: daylight, shade, **tungsten / indoor light**,
  saturated colours (foliage, sky, skin, flowers, signs). Both warm and cool light matter: the fit places two white
  points, one for each.
- Lightroom Classic (or Lightroom) with those raws in a catalogue.

## Step by step

1. **Write the baseline preset:**

   ```sh
   {{cli_binary}} calibrate-lightroom preset -o Baseline.xmp
   ```

   It sets every Basic and Presence slider to 0, sharpening and noise reduction (colour too) to 0, turns lens
   profile, chromatic aberration and vignetting corrections off, removes crops and keeps White Balance *As Shot*. It
   picks no profile. Import it in Lightroom's Develop module (Presets ▸ Import Presets…).

2. **Make virtual copies** of the photos (Photo ▸ Create Virtual Copy) so your own edits stay untouched, and apply
   the baseline preset to the copies.

3. **Choose a profile** on the copies (*Adobe Standard* is required; *Adobe Color*, *Camera Standard* and others are
   optional extras, one export each) and **export** with:
   - File format **TIFF**, **16 bits**, colour space **ProPhoto RGB**, no compression or ZIP;
   - image sizing off (full size), output sharpening off;
   - metadata **All** (the tool reads camera model, capture time and the profile name from it);
   - into **one folder per profile**, named after it, e.g. `exports/Adobe Standard/`, `exports/Adobe Color/`. Folders
     are searched with their subfolders, so you can pass just `exports` (each file's profile comes from its metadata,
     else from the folder it is in).

   Repeat step 3 for each profile you want matched.

4. **Check the set** without fitting:

   ```sh
   {{cli_binary}} calibrate-lightroom fit --raws ~/Pictures/raws --exports exports --dry-run
   ```

   It prints how many raw ↔ export pairs it found, each file it skipped and why (a different camera, a crop, a
   non-zero slider, no matching raw), the white-balance range covered and the looks found. A narrow range means you
   should add tungsten-lit or daylight photos.

5. **Fit:**

   ```sh
   {{cli_binary}} calibrate-lightroom fit --raws ~/Pictures/raws --exports exports -o out --contributor "Your Name"
   ```

   Progress goes to the terminal; a fit takes a few minutes. It writes:
   - `out/<Make>_<Model>.json`: **the file to send**;
   - `out/<Make>_<Model>.<look>.dcp`: one camera profile per look, **for your own testing only**. Import it in the
     app (command `camera.importDcp`) to see the result on your photos. Don't send or publish these.

   The tool lists exactly what the JSON holds and the held-out colour error (ΔE00 on photos the fit did not see)
   with the app's current colour and with the calibration. If the calibration doesn't beat the current colour, it
   says what failed (often: add photos under another light).

6. **Send the JSON file** where `fit` tells you to: the project's calibration thread on Discord (the link is printed),
   or an issue on the project's repository. `--contributor`
   is optional; leave it out to stay anonymous.

## Other editors: Capture One, darktable, RawTherapee, DxO PhotoLab

The same fit works against another editor's exports, so people moving from it can open their raws in its look:

```
{{cli_binary}} calibrate-lightroom fit --editor capture-one --raws RAWS --exports EXPORTS
```

`--editor` takes `capture-one`, `darktable`, `rawtherapee`, `dxo-photolab` or `lightroom` (the default). Prepare
the exports as for Lightroom: the editor's default rendering of each raw with nothing adjusted, white balance As Shot,
no crop, no lens correction, sharpening and noise reduction off where the editor allows. Export 16-bit TIFFs in a
wide colour space with its ICC profile embedded, one folder per look (the folder's name names the look). Those other
editors write no settings the fit could check, so it can't tell whether an export was adjusted: keep them at their
defaults.

What differs from a Lightroom calibration:

- The file is `<Make>_<Model>_<editor>.json` and its looks are named after the editor ("Capture One Standard"), so
  it sits beside a Lightroom calibration of the same camera without replacing it.
- Only Lightroom gives its temperature and tint in the exports, so the white points come from the camera database's
  matrices rather than from the editor.
- Its looks are offered per photo (Profile ▸ Camera profile), never as the camera's default: only a Lightroom
  calibration's first look starts every photo of the camera.

### Brightness-dependent colour (`--value-divisions`)

Some editors' looks change a colour's saturation with its brightness: Capture One's ProStandard makes dark oranges and
yellows fuller and bright ones paler than one correction per hue can. With `--value-divisions 4,6 --folds 5` the fit
also tries hue/saturation maps with that many value steps (sRGB-encoded, `ProfileHueSatMapEncoding` 1):

- Every step starts as the single-step correction; a smooth brightness-dependent part is fitted on top. It is pinned to
  the single-step correction at black, bends slowly along brightness, is pulled back to the single-step correction
  where few samples back a step, and at white holds the correction of the step below. The fit prints how many
  training samples (and how many oranges and yellows, with their L*) fall in each step.
- Each candidate and the single-step map are fitted on every fold (each fold holds out a different fifth of the
  photos). A candidate is kept only if it beats the single-step map on the mean held-out ΔE00 median and p95, loses no
  fold clearly (by more than 0.10 median or 0.25 p95), and passes the steepness rule below in every fold. Of the
  candidates that pass, the smallest wins unless a larger one's mean p95 is at least 0.15 lower. Otherwise the
  single-step map is written, and the fit says why.
- **Steepness rule.** How much the map's hue shift and saturation change per stop of brightness between neighbouring
  value steps (the step up from black is left out, as it spans unboundedly many stops). The cells of both illuminant
  maps are pooled, in two groups: oranges/yellows (hue 10–60°) and blues (hue 190–250°), saturation 0.15 and up. The
  99th percentile of each group must stay within the limit in every fold (the worst fold counts, not the mean); the
  largest change is printed but not checked. The limits are Capture One's own 99th percentile change per stop, plus
  25 %: oranges/yellows 5.75° and 0.35 (|ln saturation ratio|), blues 3.75° and 0.30. Capture One's figures were
  measured from its exports of the same photos at −1, 0 and +1 EV: **5 scenes only, and the blue reference comes from
  a single dusk sky**. The 25 % tolerance is a judgement call, and the same limits apply whichever editor the fit
  matches.
- **Near-white check.** The per-stop rule leaves out saturations below 0.15, and bright near-neutrals (clouds,
  highlights) have almost no training samples and no Capture One reference in the exposure series. A candidate must
  therefore leave near-white tints about where the same fold's single-step fit puts them: for every hue at saturation
  0–0.15 and brightness 0.7–1.0, the 99th percentile of the CIELAB a*b* distance between the two maps' outputs must
  stay within 2.0 in every fold. The limit is a judgement call (about a just-noticeable tint difference). Maps that
  failed it showed lavender and peach patches between neighbouring cloud areas.
- Every fit is also written to `OUTDIR/value-divisions/fold-K/N…` for testing.
- `--allow-steep` also writes each candidate that fails the smoothness rules (steepness or near-white) and nothing
  else, fitted on all photos (none held out), as `.dcp` files in `OUTDIR/steep`. The file name says
  `STEEP-N…-all-photos` and the profile name ends in `(N… steep, untested)`, so they import next to your other profiles.
  They are for trying by eye: no calibration file is written for them, and the default output is the same as without
  the option. `--all-photos-profiles` writes every candidate that way into `OUTDIR/all-photos`, labelled with whether
  it passes the rules.
- `--value-ending pin|fade|hold|hold-sat` (experimental) chooses how the brightness-dependent part ends at white:
  `hold` (the default) keeps the correction of the step below white, `pin` returns to the single-step correction on the
  white step, `fade` also pulls it back more strongly over the upper half of the brightness range, and `hold-sat` holds
  saturated colours and pins near-neutral ones (smoothly, fully held from saturation 0.25). Pinning makes the last step
  steep where the brights differ most from the single step, and shows as an edge at white in a smooth gradient.
  Holding near-neutrals carries a thinly supported correction up to white and fails the near-white check.
- `--value-low-sat-fade S` and `--value-bright-smooth K` (experimental) fade the brightness-dependent part toward the
  single-step correction below saturation S, and make it K times stiffer along brightness toward white. Neither fixed
  the near-white patches on the Z 8 data set.
- `--value-support-fade` (experimental) fades the brightness-dependent part toward the single-step correction where
  few samples lie nearby, as the single-step fit does with its own correction. On the Z 8 data it made the maps
  steeper per stop, not smoother.
- `--value-blue-pull` (experimental) removes the brightness-dependent part for sky blues at every saturation: input
  hue 200–240° (where cloud pixels of blue skies sit before the map), tapering smoothly to none over 15° on each
  side. On the Z 8 data it cost about 0.2 held-out ΔE00 median, and the clouds then show the single-step map's own
  tints instead of cleaner ones.
- `--table-smooth L` (experimental, also without `--value-divisions`) adds a curvature penalty to every table solve:
  squared second differences along hue and along saturation, for the hue shift, the saturation scale and the value
  scale. Each is weighted by L × the largest sample weight of the three cells it spans where all three hold data
  (so a densely sampled cell whose samples disagree with its neighbours can't keep a jump), and by L × the smallest
  where it reaches into empty cells. Rough tables turn sensor noise in smooth colour transitions (haze, sky, out-of-focus petals) into
  mottled colour; on the Z 8 data L = 320 renders those about as smoothly as a Gaussian post-smoothing of the table
  over 3 cells (L = 40 about like 1.5 cells), and lowers the held-out ΔE00 where post-smoothing raises it.
- `--photo-cap F` (experimental) lets no photo hold more than F of a table cell's sample weight (for example 0.25), so
  one photo with many samples of one colour can't decide that cell alone.

Without `--value-divisions` the fit is unchanged. The value steps index brightness as the profile sees it (before the
exposure slider and the tone curve), so the correction follows the brightness of the scene as captured.

**Known limit and future direction.** Capture One exports of the same photos at −1, 0 and +1 EV suggest that its
brightness-dependent saturation follows the brightness of the *output*, after its exposure change: the difference to a
single-step profile stays the same at every exposure when grouped by output lightness, and not when grouped by the
lightness as captured. A map with value steps sits before ChromaForge's exposure slider (as both the hue/saturation map
and the look table do), so it matches Capture One best at the exposure the photos were exported at and drifts away
from it at others. Following Capture One across exposure needs a correction applied after exposure, tied to the tone
curve's output: a renderer change, not part of this option. A likely but unverified cause is Capture One's tone curve
itself changing saturation with output brightness (as a curve applied to each RGB channel does), measured so far on
five photos only.

## Privacy: what the file holds

Only aggregate numbers: camera make and model, two colour and two forward matrices, optional hue/saturation maps,
per look an exposure offset, tone curve and optional look table, and the evidence (number of photos and samples,
photos per white-balance range, hue coverage, held-out ΔE00). The optional contributor name and the tool version.

It holds **no image content, file names, dates, serial numbers, locations or lens data**. The file is checked against
an allow-list of fields when written and again when added to the repository: a file with any other field is refused.
Anything that contains your images (the `.dcp` files' test renders, a local report) stays on your computer.

## Why this is allowed (data rules)

Maintainer decision 2026-10-25: a black-box fit to Lightroom's **output images** of a contributor's own photos may
ship, as aggregate statistics under MIT OR Apache-2.0. Adobe's own profile files (DCP, LCP), matrices read from
Adobe-converted DNGs and Adobe-rendered pixels never ship.

## For developers: adding a contribution

```sh
{{cli_binary}} calibrate-lightroom add path/to/<Make>_<Model>.json [--repo .] [--contributor "Name"] [--lens "Lens model"] [--replace]
```

`add` parses and validates the file (format version, allow-listed fields, matrix and table bounds), optionally sets
the contributor, writes the canonical form to `crates/camdb/data/calibrations/<Make>_<Model>.json` and adds (or
replaces) its row in `assets/ATTRIBUTION.md`. Commit both, with the release notes. A file that fails validation is
refused with the reason and nothing is written.

**One file per camera, lens and editor.** A fit can be for one lens: `--lens` (on `fit` or `add`) records the
photos' Exif lens model (`LensModel`, the model identifier only, never a serial number) and the file becomes
`<Make>_<Model>_<Lens>.json`. A photo uses its own lens's calibration when there is one, else the camera's lens-less
calibration, and never a calibration made for another lens; phones whose cameras share one Exif model (e.g. a main,
wide and ultrawide module) need this. `add` refuses to overwrite a calibration already there for the same camera,
lens and editor; pass `--replace` to replace it (the two fits are not merged).

Before committing, try a contribution in a debug build without adding it: point
`{{env_prefix}}_CAMERA_CALIBRATIONS` at a folder holding the JSON and open that camera's raws. `{{cli_binary}} calibrate-lightroom
list` prints the calibrations built into a binary, their looks (`cal:<id>`) and their held-out ΔE00.

### Probe DNGs and slider sweeps: `probe`, `probe-check`

Natural photos rarely show a camera's most saturated colours, which is where profiles differ most. A **probe** fills
that in, and its **slider sweeps** measure how Lightroom's sliders respond, so the import of Lightroom edits can be
tuned to match.

```
{{cli_binary}} calibrate-lightroom probe -o probes --like IMG_0001.ARW
```

1. **Write the probes.** This writes `probe_base.dng`, a grid of 384 flat colour patches spread through the camera's
   RGB (a cube, a grey ramp and saturated primaries and secondaries). It also writes one copy per sweep step with a
   single Lightroom setting embedded in its XMP: Exposure, Contrast, Highlights, Shadows, Whites, Blacks, Dehaze,
   Vibrance, Saturation, and the eight colours' Hue, Saturation and Luminance, at a few values each. The probes
   carry your camera's make and model (`--like` copies them from one of its raws; else `--make` and `--model`) and a
   colour matrix from the camera database. They also set `UniqueCameraModel` (override with `--unique-model`), which
   Lightroom uses to offer its own profiles for the camera.
2. **Render them in Lightroom.** Import the folder; Lightroom applies each probe's embedded setting on import. If
   the probes show unedited, select them all and choose Metadata ▸ Read Metadata from Files. Check that the base
   probe's profile is the one you want calibrated (Adobe Color by default), then export every probe as a 16-bit
   ProPhoto RGB TIFF, keeping the file names.
3. **Check them:**
   ```
   {{cli_binary}} calibrate-lightroom probe-check --probes probes --exports probe-exports
   ```
   It prints the default render's ΔE00 between Lightroom and the app over the patches. For every sweep step it
   prints the app control the import maps that setting to, the value the import uses, and the app value whose change
   from the base probe matches Lightroom's change best, each with its error. It compares changes rather than colours,
   so the difference between the two default looks doesn't bias the slider answer. `--json` and `-o FILE` give the
   full report.
4. **Feed the base probe to `fit`.** Add the probe folder to `fit --raws` and the folder holding `probe_base`'s
   export to `--exports`, next to your own raws and exports. It pairs by name like any raw. The sweep exports aren't
   at the baseline and are skipped.

The probes are synthetic: they contain no photo of yours and no Adobe data, and Lightroom only renders them.

### Checking edited photos: `verify`

`{{cli_binary}} calibrate-lightroom verify --raws DIR… --exports DIR…` answers "will my edited photos look the same
after the move?". Export your *edited* photos from Lightroom as 16-bit TIFFs (any profile, crop or slider settings,
**Include: All Metadata** so the export carries its develop settings). Point `verify` at the raws and at those
exports. For each pair (matched by camera model and capture time, else by file name) it does three things:

1. Imports the raw as the app would: it reads the raw's XMP sidecar, or, when the export carries its own `crs:`
   settings, uses those instead, since they are exactly what was exported.
2. Renders it in the colour space the export was written in (sRGB, Adobe RGB, Display P3, ProPhoto…), so both are
   clipped to the same gamut.
3. Measures the render against the export with `compare` (CIEDE2000).

It prints one line per photo, then a table by **group of controls** the edits used: light, tone curve, presence,
colour mixer, colour grading, white balance, profile, effects, lens, crop, masks, black & white, or default. A group
whose error stands out is the part of the import to look at. `--report DIR` writes `verify.json` and a ΔE00 map per
photo; `--json` prints the report; `--max N` checks the first N pairs; `--size N` sets the long edge compared. The
exports are only read, never changed or sent anywhere.

### Measuring a render against another editor: `compare`

`{{cli_binary}} compare REFERENCE TEST` measures how far one render of a photo is from another, from any editor or
camera (JPEG, TIFF, PNG, … read through their embedded colour profiles). It lines the two up (scale and shift, so a
small lens-correction or crop difference is fine), then prints CIEDE2000 mean, median, p95 and p99 overall, by tone
band (by the reference's L*) and by hue sector, with the test's mean lightness, chroma and hue bias. `--diff MAP.png`
writes a false-colour ΔE00 map, `--json` the full report, `--size N` sets the long edge compared (default 512) and
`--no-align` compares pixel for pixel. Use it to check a calibration on photos it did not see, or the app's render of
an edited photo against the edited export from the editor you are moving from:

```
{{cli_binary}} render IMG_0001.CR3 -o ours.tif
{{cli_binary}} compare lightroom/IMG_0001.tif ours.tif --diff map.png
```

Sources: own design; DNG specification 1.7 chapter 6 (profile model); CIEDE2000 (Sharma, Wu, Dalal 2005).
